## Findings

### High — Protected-checkpoint failures are still treated as retryable media candidates

`certify_media_candidate` records a failed post-toggle checkpoint in `post_rc` and a failed pre/post-restore checkpoint in `restore_post_rc`, but after playback is unchanged or exactly restored it returns `1`. The outer loop interprets `1` as permission to select another source and may eventually launch both hosts. This permits a completed `media-control` operation with missing/failed PID, generation, socket, or peer evidence, contrary to the requirement that every direct control be bracketed by successful checkpoints and that protected-boundary failure block the run.

Track checkpoint failure separately from media controllability. If playback may have changed, restore it first; then return the hard-blocker status (`2`) for any failed checkpoint. Status `1` should be possible only when all relevant checkpoints succeeded and the rejected candidate is proven unchanged/restored.
